FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lexer.rs:482 — bare `=` lexes as `TokenKind::Equal`, same as `==` — SPEC.md:281 documents only `==` (`if x == 10`) and docs/GRAMMAR.md:93 defines `eq_op = 'is equal to' | '=='` with no bare `=`, so `if x = 10` / `a = b` are silently accepted though previously `LexerError` and still undocumented; REPORT.md's claim of consistency with the grammar and "no previously-rejected program is now accepted" is false, pinned in by tests/comparison_lex_test.rs:109 — fix by requiring the second `=` in the `=` arm (else `LexerError`) and updating the `("=", Equal)` test expectation.
2. [MINOR] src/lexer.rs:461 — comment claims "`!=` is likewise read as `Not` followed by `Equal` by the parser" — code at src/lexer.rs:489 directly produces single `TokenKind::NotEqual` and src/parser.rs:1244 has a dedicated `NotEqual` arm, so the comment misdescribes the token stream — fix by rewriting the comment to state `!=` is one `NotEqual` token and bare `!` stays prefix `Not`.
3. [MINOR] src/formatter.rs:629 — doc comment states "`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `in` have no token at all" — false after this phase, which adds `Less`/`LessEqual`/`Greater`/`GreaterEqual` at src/lexer.rs:464 — fix by updating the comment to reflect the tokens now exist.
